<resources>
    <!-- Keep AOSP EthernetService off ARC-owned interfaces.

         The ARC container's uplink is owned end-to-end by ArcNetworkService /
         ArcNetworkAgent, which relies on the IPv4 address patchpanel already
         assigned to eth0 (100.115.92.22/30) and only brings the link up.

         AOSP's EthernetTracker matches any interface against this regex
         (default "eth\d"), grabs eth0 in CLIENT mode, and its IpClient
         (defaulting to IpAssignment.DHCP) flushes patchpanel's address, runs
         DHCP against a bridge with no server, and never restores it. That
         leaves eth0 UP at L2 with no IPv4 address and an empty routing table
         while ConnectivityService still advertises the stale ArcNetworkAgent
         network as ETHERNET/VALIDATED — apps route into a dead interface.

         Only bites VM hosts (apple/VMware/vmware), where Chrome exposes the
         host network to ARC as Ethernet (eth0). Real machines get wlan0, which
         "eth\d" never matched, so clearing the regex is safe everywhere:
         ArcNetworkService is the sole owner of eth0/wlan0. See
         docs/arc-eth0-network-ownership-race-vm.md. -->
    <string translatable="false" name="config_ethernet_iface_regex"></string>
</resources>
